\section{Compose Continuous Processes Through FIFOs}

A Kahn process network consists of deterministic sequential processes connected by unbounded, lossless FIFO channels. Reading an empty channel blocks; writing does not block in the mathematical model. A process cannot test whether a channel is empty and choose a branch from that observation. Under the continuity conditions of the model, the network denotes a least fixed point of stream equations, so relative process speeds do not alter the produced streams \cite{kahn1974process}.

This is a semantic model, not a claim that a machine has infinite memory. A runtime normally uses finite buffers and scheduling. Such an implementation is valid only while its blocking policy does not introduce a deadlock absent from the unbounded network or change channel histories. Faster producers may require unbounded growth even when every finite output prefix is well defined.

\section{Build a Determinate Merge}

The following KPN-style process syntax merges one item from each input in a fixed order:

\begin{verbatim}
process alternate(a, b -> out) {
  forever {
    x = read(a); write(out, x);
    y = read(b); write(out, y);
  }
}
\end{verbatim}

For \(a=\langle1,2,\ldots\rangle\) and \(b=\langle10,20,\ldots\rangle\), the output is \(\langle1,10,2,20,\ldots\rangle\), regardless of which producer runs faster. Replacing the body by ``read whichever input is ready'' adds a non-Kahn observation and can make the output schedule-dependent. \Cref{fig:visual-355} marks the only legal communication paths.

