\section{Execute a Finite Prefix}

\begin{verbatim}
while requested_output_prefix_not_ready:
    p := choose_runnable_process_fairly()
    run p until its next blocking read
    append each write to the destination FIFO
    if no process is runnable:
        report implementation deadlock or missing external input
\end{verbatim}

Running to the next blocking read is one implementation grain; a runtime may preempt earlier. Fairness avoids permanently starving a runnable process, but it cannot manufacture a finite buffer bound.

\section{Lower Networks Without Weakening Channels}

The IR should have process nodes, typed FIFO edges with one writer and one reader, process control state, explicit external I/O effects, and provenance for process code and channel endpoints. Channel rates or clocks are generally unknown rather than silently equal to one; optional inferred bounds must be labeled as proofs or assumptions. Compilation can produce threads and queues, coroutine code with a scheduler, a sequentialized network, or communicating hardware blocks.

Legal optimizations include process fusion and channel elimination when they preserve blocking reads, FIFO order, and external effects; batching is also legal if no process can observe the changed timing. Semantic-preservation risks include finite-buffer insertion, scheduler-dependent selection, and reordering writes across effects, any of which can introduce deadlock or change streams. Compare finite output prefixes and effect traces under the same external inputs; do not treat one successful schedule as proof for all schedules.

\section{Interpret Waiting Without Inspecting Emptiness}

Offer \(a_0,a_1\) on \(a\) and delay the first item on \(b\). The alternating merge emits \(a_0\), then waits. It cannot consume \(a_1\) as a substitute for \(b_0\); otherwise the result would depend on when a scheduler ran the producer for \(b\). Once \(b_0\) becomes available the merge emits it, then emits \(a_1\) and blocks on \(b_1\). Whether the system can finish depends on the external input protocol. The absence of a token in the runtime queue is not an end-of-stream indication.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Next request & Queue state & Legal action \\
\hline
Read \(a\) & \(a_0,a_1\) pending & Read \(a_0\) \\
Read \(b\) & \(b\) empty & Suspend on \(b\) \\
Read \(b\) & \(b_0\) arrives & Read \(b_0\) \\
Read \(a\) & \(a_1\) still pending & Read \(a_1\) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{verbatim}
resume(process):
    for step in 1 .. quantum:
        op := next_instruction(process)
        if op is read and channel(op).is_empty():
            register_waiter(process, channel(op))
            return BLOCKED              # same instruction on resume
        perform(op)
        advance_instruction_pointer(process)
    enqueue_at_end_of_ready_queue(process)
\end{verbatim}

A quantum is necessary in this sketch: an infinite writer might never encounter a blocking read. A queued reader must also be awakened when a writer supplies the required token; a concurrent runtime must avoid a lost wakeup between testing and registering. Neither scheduling detail should be visible to source code. A useful test runs schedules in which \(b_0\) is queued first and last, respectively. After both arrivals the same prefix must be available. Before \(b_0\) arrives, the merge's output ending at \(a_0\) is not a competing answer; it is a prefix of the same answer.

\paragraph{Choose an explicit end-of-stream protocol.}
The process as written has no termination instruction. To turn it into a
finite batch operator, for example, use tagged data and end tokens and
define what happens when the first end token is read. Stopping there,
draining the other channel, or reporting unequal input lengths are three
different programs. A draining version still has to specify the order of
the remaining data and the position of its output end marker.
Try two inputs of unequal lengths and one input whose first item is
delayed. Neither can be handled correctly by a rule that uses an empty
queue as the end marker: the delayed case is merely a different arrival
schedule of the same input stream.
